\caption{Deployed concurrent authentication architecture. The uploaded login video feeds two independent branches evaluated in parallel: a biometric branch that reduces the recording to one login template and scores face and voice against the enrolled encrypted template, and a challenge branch that transcribes the audio and verifies it against the issued CAPTCHA. The enrolled reference is an AES-256-GCM encrypted, pseudonymously named template, never a stored recording. The three verdicts are combined by the conjunctive rule of equation~(\ref{eq:fusion}); access is granted only when all three pass, and the response is identical whichever factor fails.}
\label{fig:concurrent}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Feature Representations}\label{sec:features}
The scheme is agnostic to the specific matchers; the representations it instantiates for the face and voice factors are described below.

\emph{Face.} The face factor uses face detection followed by a 512-dimensional deep embedding trained with an additive angular margin objective \cite{arcface}, compared by cosine similarity as in equation~(\ref{eq:face}). On the recordings available the embedding scores 0.7265 and 0.6194 for genuine pairs and 0.2247 and 0.1838 for impostor pairs against a threshold $\tau_f = 0.35$, separating the two populations on this data. These are a handful of pairs, not an accuracy evaluation, and no false-acceptance or false-rejection rate is claimed from them. Twelve frames are sampled uniformly across each recording; the enrolled reference is the L2-normalized mean of the per-frame embeddings.

\emph{Voice.} Text-independent speaker verification requires a discriminative learned embedding, so the voice factor uses a 192-dimensional ECAPA-TDNN speaker embedding \cite{ecapa} scored by cosine similarity as in equation~(\ref{eq:voice}), which is structurally the same decision the face factor makes. The operating threshold $\tau_a = 0.30$ is taken from a measured cosine distribution rather than assumed: over the labelled window pairs available, real cross-speaker pairs reached at most 0.256 while genuine same-speaker pairs floored at 0.345, and 0.30 lies in that gap. That distribution rests on two speakers, so it fixes a defensible operating point on the available data; it is not an equal-error-rate calibration and no error rate is claimed for it.

\subsection{Security Mechanisms}\label{sec:security}
Four mechanisms carry the security argument of Section~\ref{sec:contrib}.

\emph{Template-only storage.} No recording of any user is retained. The registration video is reduced to the two embeddings above and destroyed; only the embeddings are stored. This matters because an embedding is not a playable recording of the subject's face and voice, so a breach of the enrolment store does not yield reusable biometric media. The stored artefact is also two orders of magnitude smaller than the recording it replaces.

\emph{Encryption at rest with integrity.} Templates are sealed under AES-256-GCM. The authenticated-encryption tag doubles as an integrity check: a template modified on disk fails authentication during decryption, and the scheme fails closed by rejecting the login rather than proceeding with unverified data. The login recording, which must persist briefly because two independent branches consume it, is encrypted under the same envelope and deleted once the attempt resolves, whatever its outcome.

\emph{Pseudonymous storage (unlinkability).} Confidentiality and integrity protect a template's \emph{contents}, but they say nothing about what its \emph{name} discloses. If a stored template is named after the account it belongs to, then a compromise of the storage directory alone---without the database and without the encryption key---reveals the entire enrolled membership by name and yields one labelled biometric template per person, each reusable for cross-system correlation. This is the unlinkability concern of ISO/IEC~24745. Each template is therefore stored under a name that is a keyed hash of the identifier, $\mathrm{HMAC}(k,\text{id})$ under the same secret $k$ that protects the templates, rather than under the identifier itself. A keyed hash rather than a plain one is deliberate: identifiers are low-entropy and would fall to a dictionary against an unkeyed digest, whereas an adversary who lacks $k$ cannot map a stored name back to an identity, and one who possesses $k$ can already decrypt the templates, so no additional linkage is conceded. The mapping is deterministic, so verification re-derives the name at login without any stored lookup table, and no plaintext identifier is written to the template store.

\emph{Scope of the unlinkability property.} This property applies to the biometric template store and should be read accordingly, including the \textit{Unlinkable} rating in Table~\ref{tab:2}. It does not extend to the account database, which is a separate store. Authentication necessarily requires an account record, and that record holds, in plaintext, the account information supplied at registration---a unique username and email, and optionally profile fields such as name, mobile number and address---because the username must be queried on every login and the remaining fields are functional account data rather than biometric material. The two stores are decoupled: the account database contains no template and no template file name, while the template store contains no plaintext identifier, so the templates are pseudonymously identified and stored separately from the account information. A compromise limited to the template directory therefore discloses neither identities nor a mapping from a stored template to one, which is the property claimed; a compromise of the account database discloses the enrolled identities and their contact details but no biometric template. Unlinkability is thus a property of the biometric template store, not of the account database, and hardening the account record---for instance by encrypting its contact fields under a key distinct from $k$, or by minimising the fields collected---is an orthogonal deployment measure that does not affect the biometric protections described here.

\emph{Enrolment binding.} The identity being enrolled is established by a short-lived server-signed enrolment token issued once, at account creation, and never by client-supplied data such as a filename. This closes an account-takeover path: if the enrolled identity is read from the uploaded filename, any unauthenticated client can replace another user's stored template with its own biometrics and then authenticate as that user. Because account creation rejects duplicate identifiers, a token can never be minted for an account that already exists, so an existing enrolment cannot be overwritten. A session token is explicitly not accepted for enrolment, so a stolen session cannot re-key an account.

\emph{Challenge handling.} The challenge is generated server-side and only its rendered image is transmitted; the expected string never reaches the client, since a client that knows the expected text can satisfy the challenge without a live utterance. Each challenge is bound to one identifier, is consumed on the first verification attempt whether or not that attempt succeeds, and expires after a fixed window of five minutes. A login recording is accepted for upload only while an issued, unexpired challenge exists for that identifier, so a recording cannot be submitted outside a live login attempt. The challenge alphabet is restricted to five decimal digits. This is a deliberate consequence of the recognition stage rather than a security choice: transcription of arbitrary letter strings proved unreliable, whereas digit sequences transcribe dependably, and the comparison of equation~(\ref{eq:captcha}) normalizes spelled-out number words to numerals before matching so that a correct utterance is not rejected for orthographic reasons. The reduced alphabet lowers the entropy of a single challenge, which is acceptable only because the challenge is not a standalone credential: it is conjoined with two biometric factors under equation~(\ref{eq:fusion}), and guessing it confers nothing without also passing them.

\emph{Input validation.} Every identifier that reaches a filesystem path or a subprocess argument is validated against a restrictive character class at account creation, at enrolment and at login, and the uploaded filename is pattern-checked rather than trusted. Without this, an identifier containing path separators would escape the intended storage directory, and the enrolled identity is in any case taken from the signed token rather than the filename.


The proposed authentication scheme's registration phase considers Email address and unique username. After this, the user records the real-time video while speaking the dynamic CAPTCHA. This recording is reduced to the biometric templates defined in Section~\ref{sec:features}, which are encrypted and stored; the recording itself is discarded and is never written to persistent storage.
The proposed authentication system uses biometric features of face and voice. The user is required to capture a real-time video. However, while recording the video, the user must speak the dynamic CAPTCHA displayed on the screen. From this real-time recorded video, face, voice and CAPTCHA are extracted. The corresponding reference is not a stored recording but the encrypted template enrolled for that identifier, which was derived once at registration and retained in place of the video. Matching therefore compares the login embeddings against the enrolled embeddings, never against a second recording. Also, the voice/audio is converted to text and matched with the dynamic CAPTCHA text displayed on the screen. All these matching results are then applied AND logic, which ensures positive authentication only if all the results are positive. These results are combined to decide whether the authentication is successful and give the final decision. Dynamic CAPTCHA reduces video reuse/replay attacks and adds to the liveliness detection of a specific user.

 Initially, we start with entering a unique username that helps in user identification and then a real-time video capturing field and dynamic CAPTCHA is displayed. The individual trying to authenticate themself records real-time video while speaking dynamic CAPTCHA, displayed on the screen. From this recorded video, frames are extracted. Also, audio is extracted from this recorded video. The per-frame embeddings computed from these frames are scored against the enrolled face embedding stored for that identifier.
Similarly, the speaker embedding computed from the login audio is scored against the enrolled speaker embedding to decide whether the person speaking is the enrolled user. No registration audio exists on the server to be re-extracted. Also, the extracted audio is converted to text matched with the CAPTCHA displayed for CAPTCHA validation checking. After matching frames and audio is complete, the matching score is checked to see if it is above a certain threshold and matching is successful. Then, all these results are subjected to AND logic, which gives positive results only if all validations and matching are positive. The user is granted access to resources if the result is positive. Otherwise, access is not granted.

There are three phases involved in this authentication scheme - 
\begin{itemize}
    \item Registration Phase
    \item Login Phase
    \item Authentication phase
\end{itemize}
\subsection{Registration Phase}
