Following steps are involved in the registration phase:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Initially, the user enters their personal information - Email ID username. The username 
 and Email ID needs to be unique for each individual.
 \item In the next step, a dynamic CAPTCHA is displayed on the screen, and a video recording interface is presented. The user has to record their video while speaking the CAPTCHA showed. The challenge shown at registration is generated on the client and serves only to elicit natural speech for the voice template; it is not verified, and no security property is claimed for it. The challenge is a verified factor at login only, where it is server-generated, withheld from the client, and checked against the transcript by equation~(\ref{eq:captcha}).
 \item On clicking the start recording button, the video recording will start. On clicking stop recording, the recorded video is available for preview. 
 \item On clicking save, the recording is uploaded and immediately reduced on the server to the face and voice templates. The templates are encrypted under AES-256-GCM and stored as the enrolment reference; the uploaded recording is held only in memory and in a temporary file that is deleted once extraction completes. If no usable face can be extracted, no template is stored and the enrolment is rejected, so an account can never reach a state where it has an unusable reference
 \item On clicking the register button, the user is registered successfully if the submitted credentials pass all the checks implied. Users can further update their information like address, full name, etc.
\end{enumerate}
\emph{The registration-page screenshot previously shown here has been withdrawn. It depicted a \texttt{save} button that the current client no longer has, and it showed an identifiable participant. The registration workflow is specified in the enumerated steps above and in Fig.~\ref{fig:4}.}



\subsection{Login Phase}
The following steps are involved in the login phase:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item The user enters the username submitted at registration. If the username does not match that submitted at registration, we cannot proceed further.
    \item A dynamic CAPTCHA is also displayed on the screen. This is server-side CAPTCHA, which is dynamic.
    \item The User trying to log in needs to record a video while speaking the CAPTCHA displayed.
    \item On clicking the start recording button, the video recording will start. On clicking stop recording, the recorded video is available for preview.
    \item On clicking save, the login recording is encrypted and written to a transient location, because two independent verification branches consume it. It is deleted as soon as the attempt resolves, so no login recording is retained either.
\end{enumerate}
