\subsection{Authentication Phase}
The following steps are involved in the authentication phase:
\noindent The enrolled reference is a template, not a stored recording, and the three factors are evaluated concurrently:
\begin{enumerate}
\item The submitted identifier is checked against the enrolled accounts.
\item The challenge issued to this identifier is retrieved and consumed, so that it cannot be reused by a subsequent attempt regardless of the outcome of this one.
\item The enrolled template and the freshly uploaded login recording are decrypted into a private working directory. Failure to decrypt or authenticate either artefact rejects the attempt immediately.
\item The login recording is reduced once to a login template, yielding its per-frame face embeddings and its speaker embedding.
\item The face and voice factors are scored against the enrolled template concurrently, by equations~(\ref{eq:face}) and~(\ref{eq:voice}).
\item Concurrently with the above, the challenge branch demultiplexes the login audio, transcribes it, and evaluates equation~(\ref{eq:captcha}). This branch depends only on the login audio, so it does not wait on the face verdict.
\item The three verdicts are combined by the conjunctive rule of equation~(\ref{eq:fusion}). On success a signed session token is issued; on failure the response is identical irrespective of which factor failed.
\item The working directory and the login recording are destroyed, whatever the outcome.
\end{enumerate}

\renewcommand{\algorithmicrequire}{\textbf{Input:}}
\renewcommand{\algorithmicensure}{\textbf{Output:}}


\section{Implementation and Analysis}
\subsection{Experimental Setup}
A proof-of-concept web application was implemented to validate the feasibility of the proposed scheme under real-time acquisition. The prototype follows a client--server separation of concerns: the client is responsible only for acquisition and for presenting the server-issued challenge, while enrolment references, challenge generation, feature extraction, matching and the fusion decision of equation~(\ref{eq:fusion}) are executed on the server. Keeping the decision logic server-side is a security requirement rather than an implementation detail, since a client-side decision could be bypassed by a modified client.
\subsubsection{Hardware Requirements}\label{sec:hw}
The Laptop used for experimenting was an Intel(R) Core(TM) i5-4210U CPU @ 1.70GHz   2.40 GHz with 8.00 GB installed RAM. The system was a 64-bit operating system, x64-based processor. The system was running Windows Operating system with edition Windows 10 Education, version 22H2, and OS build 19045.2965. The website was hosted on localhost in Chrome browser Version 114.0.5735.110 (Official Build) (64-bit). The functional behaviour of the scheme was verified on this machine. The timing measurements reported in Section~\ref{sec:perf} were collected on a separate host---an Intel Core i9-12900 (24 threads) with 62\,GB of RAM, running the server runtime and a local database instance---because the biometric pipeline is model-load bound and timings taken on the first machine would not be representative of the deployed configuration. The two machines are therefore not interchangeable, and the latency figures should be read against the second one.
\subsubsection{ Prototype Architecture}
The prototype is organised as a three-tier architecture. The presentation tier is a single-page browser client that performs media capture and renders the challenge; the application tier is an event-driven server that generates the per-session CAPTCHA, orchestrates the extraction and matching pipeline and evaluates the fusion rule; and the persistence tier stores the enrolment reference associated with each unique identifier. The processing pipeline itself is realised through four functional components, each of which is independent of the specific library that implements it: a demultiplexing component that separates the video and audio streams of the acquired recording \cite{s1}, a spectral analysis component that derives the audio feature representation \cite{s3}, a speech-to-text component that produces the transcript used for challenge validation \cite{s2}, and a challenge generation component that issues an unpredictable server-side CAPTCHA per session \cite{s4}. This decomposition is deliberate: because the components interact only through the representations defined in Section~III, an individual matcher can be replaced by a stronger one without altering the system model or the decision rule. The prototype was realised as a JavaScript web application with a document-oriented store, with the media processing components invoked from the application tier. For reproducibility the concrete stack is recorded here: a React single-page client, an Express server on Node.js~22, MongoDB for account records, and Python~3.12 for the biometric pipeline, with the face and speaker models executed on CPU through ONNX Runtime and PyTorch respectively. The pinned dependency set is distributed with the implementation.


\subsection{Processing and Communication Overhead}\label{sec:perf}
A multimodal scheme is only deployable if the additional verification it performs does not dominate the interaction, so the per-stage and end-to-end costs were measured rather than estimated. Each stage was timed over $N=5$ runs of a genuine login, on the machine described in Section~\ref{sec:hw}. All five runs returned a successful authentication with all three factors positive, so the timings are for the full success path and not for an early rejection.

\begin{table}[!htbp]
\caption{Measured per-stage processing time (ms, $N=5$)}
\label{tab:stages}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|r|r|r|}
\hline
\textbf{Stage} & \textbf{Mean} & \textbf{Min} & \textbf{Max} \\ \hline
Login template extraction & 12424 & 11888 & 12779 \\
Face matching & 98 & 73 & 116 \\
Voice matching & 104 & 83 & 138 \\
Audio demultiplexing & 154 & 146 & 162 \\
Speech-to-text & 2367 & 2085 & 2666 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Template extraction dominates every other stage by two orders of magnitude. The reason is architectural rather than algorithmic: it is the one stage that loads both model stacks---the face embedding network and the speaker embedding network---into a freshly created interpreter and then runs both. Matching itself is negligible on both factors, because each is a single inner product between stored vectors.

\begin{table}[!htbp]
\caption{End-to-end authentication latency (ms, $N=5$)}
\label{tab:e2e}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|r|r|r|}
\hline
\textbf{Pipeline} & \textbf{Mean} & \textbf{Min} & \textbf{Max} \\ \hline
Concurrent (deployed) & 13293 & 12571 & 13700 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

End-to-end authentication completes in approximately 13.3\,s on the machine of Section~\ref{sec:hw}. The cost is dominated by template extraction: the three factors are evaluated concurrently, and the challenge branch (roughly 2521\,ms of demultiplexing and transcription) is far shorter than the 12424\,ms extraction it runs alongside, so it is largely hidden. Further latency reduction must therefore come from amortising model loading---a resident model service rather than a fresh process per authentication---and not from additional concurrency. This is a deployment change rather than an algorithmic one and was not attempted here.

On communication cost, the client uploads one recording per attempt. The server retains: a stored template of approximately 147\,KB in place of an 11.18\,MB recording, roughly a 76-fold reduction, with a fixed 34-byte overhead for the encryption envelope. Because the authenticated cipher mode used is a stream mode, ciphertext length equals plaintext length and encryption adds no further storage cost. Upload times over a range of uplink bandwidths can be derived from the recording size but were not measured over a live link, and are therefore not reported as experimental results.



\subsection{Evaluation Scope and Limitations}\label{sec:limits}
Because the claims of this paper should be read against the evidence that supports them, the scope of the evaluation is stated explicitly.

\emph{What was measured.} Three things were measured directly. First, the processing and communication overhead of Section~\ref{sec:perf}, over repeated runs of a genuine authentication on a single machine. Second, the score behaviour of the face and voice matchers on a small set of recordings, reported in Section~\ref{sec:features}. Third, the functional behaviour of the complete scheme, exercised end to end through the browser client against the deployed server.

\emph{What was not measured.} No recognition-accuracy evaluation was performed. The paper reports no false acceptance rate, no false rejection rate, no equal error rate, and no receiver operating characteristic, and no claim in it should be read as resting on such a metric. The decision thresholds were fixed from observed score separations on the available recordings, which is sufficient to place an operating point but is not a calibration against a target error rate.

\emph{Why the evaluation is bounded.} The enrolment corpus is small, and it is worth being precise about how small. The stored templates correspond to three distinct human identities. This admits exactly one genuine pairing---two separate recordings of the same person---and three impostor pairings between different people. A corpus of this size cannot support an error-rate estimate with a meaningful confidence interval, and we do not offer one. Two enrolments in the corpus are also thin, yielding only two frames containing a detectable face, which is a realistic failure mode of unconstrained capture and one that a larger study should characterise rather than exclude.

\emph{A consequence of the storage design.} The scheme's template-only storage, described in Section~\ref{sec:security} as a privacy property, has a methodological cost that should be acknowledged: because enrolment recordings are destroyed by design, the measurements taken from them cannot be recomputed later from the retained data. Reproducing them requires re-collecting the recordings. A study intending to publish accuracy results should therefore retain a separately consented evaluation corpus outside the production store, rather than relying on the deployment's own enrolments.

\emph{Reproducing the missing evaluation.} The accuracy study absent from this paper is automated rather than merely deferred. The implementation ships an evaluation harness that enumerates a corpus, extracts one template per recording using the deployed extraction path, verifies that no two recordings produce an identical template, scores every genuine and impostor pair with the deployed matchers, and emits false-acceptance and false-rejection curves, an equal error rate, and detection-error-tradeoff plots. Genuine pairs are formed only between different sessions of the same participant, never a recording against itself. The harness refuses to emit metrics from a corpus that cannot support them, which is why no numbers are reported here. Running it requires participant recordings that do not presently exist: at minimum five participants, and at least two separate sessions each, so that within-person variation across sessions is represented rather than assumed. A corpus of twenty to fifty participants with two or three sessions apiece would place the resulting rates on a footing comparable with the schemes surveyed in Section~II.

\emph{Collecting the corpus without a second protocol.} The template-only storage that gives the scheme its privacy property also erases, by design, the very recordings an accuracy study needs, since each recording is reduced to a template and discarded. To avoid a separate hand-managed collection step, the implementation provides an optional, off-by-default capture mode. When it is enabled, a participant using the application in the ordinary way---registering once and then logging in---has each uploaded recording additionally retained under the naming convention the harness consumes, with the registration providing one session and each login another, so that the different-session recordings required for genuine pairs arise from normal use rather than from a staged protocol. Because retention reverses the guarantee shown to the user, the mode is gated on \emph{per-upload consent}: the capture page displays an explicit retention notice, retention is opt-in, and the server retains a recording only when consent accompanied that specific upload, so enabling the mode never silently retains anyone who declined. Retained recordings are named by the same keyed hash used for templates, so a leak of the corpus listing discloses no identifiers; the recordings themselves remain identifying by content and must be handled under the participants' consent, and the mode is never enabled in a deployment.

\emph{What this means for the comparison in Table~\ref{tab:2}.} Several schemes discussed in Section~II report equal error rates on established datasets. No comparison against those figures is made or implied here, since the corresponding quantity has not been measured for this scheme. The comparison offered is the framework comparison of Table~\ref{tab:2}, which is analytic in nature, and the contribution claimed is one of architecture, storage design and security mechanism rather than one of recognition accuracy.


\subsection{Analysis of the proposed scheme}
We have used the Bonneau et al. framework \cite{IEEEtran64} to analyse our proposed scheme. This comparison is done to access our scheme in terms of usability, deployability and security in comparison to existing authentication schemes. 
We have included Uname-Passwd in our comparison as it is the most easy form of authentication and preferred by normal users. Hardware token based schemes like Yubikey U2F and FIDO2 are being compared because they offer more security although they have overhead of being carried everytime and costly. Xie et al.'s CamAuth, a two factor authentication scheme which prevents various attacks Like MITM and phishing attacks and considered secure web authentication scheme is also considered for comparison. \cite{IEEEtran30} is a MFA scheme included to compare our scheme's benefits against MFA schemes. Next we compare our scheme against other multimodal biometric authentication schemes like \cite{IEEEtran63}, \cite{r1}, \cite{r4} already existing in the literature. This is described in the table \ref{tab:2} given below:
