    The ratings in this table are analytic, following the framework's own methodology; they are reasoned from each scheme's design and are not empirical measurements. In particular the security ratings for the proposed scheme are not backed by a recognition-accuracy study, and the entries that would depend on one are argued from the conjunctive decision rule rather than from measured error rates. The \emph{No-trusted-Third-Party} rating for the proposed scheme is revised downward: the challenge factor transcribes the spoken utterance through an external speech-to-text service, so the login audio leaves the verifier's trust boundary and a third party is relied upon for the availability and correctness of that factor. Replacing it with an on-premises recogniser would restore the benefit and is identified as future work. A registration acknowledgement email is additionally dispatched through a hosted SMTP provider; it is an auxiliary notification sent on a best-effort basis after an account is created and is not part of the authentication workflow, so it does not bear on this rating, though a self-hosted mail relay would remove even that auxiliary dependency. The \emph{Unlinkable} rating refers to the biometric template store, whose file names are keyed hashes; it does not assert unlinkability of the separate account database, which is discussed in Section~\ref{sec:security}.}
    \label{tab:2}
