\section{Conclusions}
This paper proposes a multimodal biometric-based authentication system that enhances usability by allowing parallel acquisition of biometric samples. This scheme relates to a user authentication system based on a dynamic CAPTCHA and a real-time single Video input to develop a multimodal biometric authentication system. Through our research, we have achieved the following key findings:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item We are not aware of a prior scheme that combines parallel acquisition of multiple biometric samples from a single sensor with a challenge-bound liveness cue, and we have designed and implemented a scheme which provides both.
    \item The schemes already present in the literature in the field of multimodal biometrics are costly due to the use of expensive devices, which hinders their applicability by users. Our scheme is cost-effective.
\end{enumerate}
The scheme was assessed along two axes: qualitatively against the Bonneau et al. framework, which situates it with respect to existing usability, deployability and security benefits, and quantitatively through the processing-delay measurements of Section~\ref{sec:perf}. Neither axis characterises recognition accuracy, which is treated as a limitation below.\\
The limitations of this work are set out in full in Section~\ref{sec:limits} and are summarised here. Recognition accuracy is not characterised: no false acceptance, false rejection or equal error rate is reported, and the thresholds in use fix an operating point on a small corpus rather than calibrating against a target error rate. The timing results come from repeated runs of one account on a single machine, and their dominant stage is a model-loading cost that a resident model service would largely remove. The liveness argument rests on the unpredictability of the challenge rather than on an explicit presentation-attack detector. The challenge factor depends on an external speech-to-text service, which places the login audio outside the verifier's trust boundary. Reporting error rates over a multi-subject corpus, adding a presentation-attack detection stage, and substituting an on-premises recogniser are the natural directions for future work.
The frame- and audio-extraction stages and the speech-to-text stage, previously shown as implementation screenshots, are described in the text of this section and in Algorithm~\ref{alg:extract}: frames are sampled uniformly across the recording, the audio track is demultiplexed from the same recording, and the login audio is transcribed for challenge validation. The matcher outputs, previously shown as terminal listings, are consolidated in Table~\ref{tab:scores}.

